# Everything below is GENERATED by running this project. None of it belongs in
# git — it is either large, machine-specific, or a snapshot of a site that will
# have changed by the time anyone reads it. A single 3-page run drops
# ~5 MB of HTML snapshots alone.

# --- scrape output -------------------------------------------------------
*.json
*.csv
!package.json
# Committed on purpose: a real 5-row excerpt of a real run, so a reader can see
# the exact output shape without installing anything.
!sample_output.json
!sample_output.csv
!sample_output_vehicle.json
!sample_output_vehicle.csv
!fixtures_generated.json
autotrader_rows.*
autotrader_rows_*.*

# --- the live-test harness's working directory ---------------------------
live_results/
live_results.zip
# ANY working directory a live run writes into, by KIND rather than by the one
# name an older repo used (CLAUDE.md §24). Measured in a sibling repo:
# `live/` was not listed, and a `--dump-html` of a 3-page run wrote
# `canary_dump.json.page3`, which `*.json` does not cover. It sat untracked
# and NOT ignored, one `git add -A` from a commit, until the credential scan
# flagged the 32-hex ids inside it. Here `--dump-html x --pages 3` writes
# `x.page1`..`x.page3`, covered by `*.page[0-9]*` below.
live/
out/
output/
run/
runs/
result/
results/
tmp/
scratch/
dump/
dumps/

# --- debug artifacts the engines drop next to themselves -----------------
# Written on purpose when a page yields nothing or comes back blocked, and by
# --dump-html on success too. Large, and stale within hours.
*_debug.html
*_debug.png
# A one-page `--dump-html page.html`, which the README itself suggests,
# writes the served page at the repo root under the name given. A served
# page carries the address that fetched it (`clientIp`) and a session id,
# and the repo has no HTML of its own at the root, so every root .html is
# ignored.
/*.html
*.html.page*
*.json.page*
*.page[0-9]*

# --- captures kept for parser work ---------------------------------------
# The smoke suite's fixtures are committed (trimmed, in fixtures_generated.json);
# the multi-megabyte originals they were cut from are not.
captures/

# --- the landing page and the proxy list ---------------------------------
# Both live outside the repo on purpose: the landing page is published
# elsewhere, and a downloaded 2captcha proxy list is a credential file.
index.html
proxylist.txt

# --- caches --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.fingerprint_cache/

# --- packaging build artifacts --------------------------------------------
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# --- environments --------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- editors / OS --------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- never, under any circumstances --------------------------------------
# The scrapers mask user:pass in their own logs, but raw HTML dumps and the
# Scraper API's x-debug header are NOT masked, and a key pasted into a shell
# history file is not masked either.
*.log
.env
*.key
secrets*

# Any .env variant, however it was renamed. `.env` alone is not enough, and
# this has cost the family twice: craigslist-scraper's own .gitignore records
# a `.env.hold` made while testing that was committed WITH A LIVE KEY in it,
# recoverable only because that repo was private at the time. On 2026-09-17
# a sibling had an untracked `.env.bak` holding a 32-hex key in a PUBLIC
# repository, one `git add -A` from publication. Craigslist was fixed then
# and the fix never reached anyone else, which is §16's rule unapplied: when
# you fix one, check the siblings.
.env*
!.env.example
